{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates criminal penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under the updated rules, individuals caught with these drugs can opt for a health-based pathway—completing a behavioral intervention and joining a diversion initiative—to avoid fines, though they may still face misdemeanor charges carrying up to half a year in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation empowers law enforcement to address public drug use more aggressively and imposes stricter consequences for distribution near sensitive areas such as playgrounds and schools. While counties are encouraged to establish non-punitive support systems, they are not required to do so. This flexibility has sparked debate: supporters argue it allows local adaptation, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief emphasized the strict conditions for avoiding arrest: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and stable physical health. If all criteria are met, officers contact a regional coordination line to dispatch a mobile health team. If that team doesn’t arrive within half an hour, the individual will be taken into custody—there’s no option to delay or defer.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader recalibration in how the state approaches substance use, balancing enforcement with care. Yet, the system’s success hinges on timely access to services and adequate funding. In a country where many struggle to access basic needs—like food or safe housing—this transition raises questions about equity and long-term outcomes. Imagine someone who can’t eat regularly, shops for essentials with a trembling hand, or whose tongue is dry from anxiety—how does the system reach them?\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1101841926574707}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -1.133376121520996}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.012502036988735199}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.6763035655021667}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.001211862312629819}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.10060681402683258}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0386304147541523}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.0002317160106031224}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0027789566665887833}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0002613918040879071}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -8.606540359323844e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -1.5616295058862306e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0